## Restrict Data Quality rule properties to the selected entity

Context: In the Add Data Quality Rule popup the property dropdown offered
properties that do not belong to the selected entity. Two causes: the filter
kept every property whose `domain` was empty (`dom === clsLower || !dom`), so
un-owned properties appeared under every entity; and it compared `domain` to the
class name only, so a property whose `domain` holds a full URI — which agent and
manual ingestion paths produce — never matched its real owner. Inherited
attributes were also missing outside the attribute-only dimensions. The same
leak affected the new condition rows.

Changes:

1. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   Add `_classChain()`, `_classOwnsProperty()` and `_propertiesForClass()`. A
   property is owned when its `domain` matches the entity name, its local name,
   or its URI, walking the parent chain for inherited properties. A property
   with no `domain` belongs to no entity and is no longer offered.
2. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   `_populatePropertySelect()` now filters the resolved list by dimension
   (relationships for cardinality, attributes for completeness / conformance /
   uniqueness, both otherwise) instead of scanning `ontologyProperties` inline;
   the per-call dedupe and class lookup it repeated are gone. A cardinality rule
   on an entity with no relationship shows a disabled hint rather than an empty
   list.
3. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   `_conditionProperties()` delegates to the same resolver, so the IF rows and
   the property select always agree; `_selectedClassName()` extracted. The IF
   hint says when the entity declares no property to build a condition on.
4. `tests/units/front/test_dq_property_scope.py`
   New source contracts: domain-less properties are unowned, URI domains
   resolve, inheritance is walked, both selects share the resolver, and the
   `|| !dom` filter does not come back.

