## Fix empty attribute dropdown for entities without materialised dataProperties

Context: After scoping the Data Quality properties to the selected entity, the
attribute dropdowns listed attributes for `Customer` but were empty for other
entities. `Customer` carries its attributes in `class.dataProperties`, which the
resolver always classifies as attributes. Every other entity relies on the
top-level `ontology.properties` list, and there `_isObjectProperty()` fell back
to *relationship* whenever a property declared neither `type` nor `range` —
exactly the shape the entity panel writes. The attributes-only dimensions then
filtered all of them out. `ontology-swrl.js` already used the opposite default,
which is why the SWRL editor listed those attributes correctly.

Changes:

1. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   Invert the fallback in `_isObjectProperty()`: a property is a relationship
   only on positive evidence — an explicit `ObjectProperty` type, or a `range`
   naming a known entity. A property with no type and no range is an attribute.
2. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   Add `_isKnownClassName()`, matching a range against entity names, URIs and
   URI local names, so an untyped relationship whose range points at an entity
   stays out of the attribute list.
3. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-dataquality.js`
   `_populatePropertySelect()` shows the disabled "No <kind> declared on
   <Entity>" hint for every dimension, not just cardinality, so a genuinely
   empty entity is legible instead of silent.
4. `tests/units/front/test_dq_property_scope.py`
   Contracts for the new classification: an untyped property is an attribute, a
   range naming an entity is a relationship, and relationship is no longer the
   fallback.

