## Behavioural test coverage for the Data Quality rule checks

Context: The Data Quality frontend was covered only by source-contract tests
that grep the JavaScript. That style pins the shape of the code but cannot run
it, which is why the `_isObjectProperty()` misclassification shipped unnoticed.
The condition translators in `ShapeConditions` were likewise only reached
indirectly, through `SHACLService`, leaving the operator vocabulary, the value
quoting and the SPARQL target untested.

Changes:

1. `tests/units/front/test_dq_property_resolver.py`
   New module that executes `ontology-dataquality.js` under node against a
   fixture ontology mixing every ingestion shape: attributes materialised in
   `dataProperties`, attributes known only as top-level properties (with a name
   domain and with a URI domain), a typed relationship, an untyped relationship
   recognisable only by its range, a domain-less property, an entity declaring
   nothing, and a parent cycle. Covers `_propertiesForClass`, `_isObjectProperty`
   and the rendered `_populatePropertySelect` options per quality dimension.
   Skipped when node is unavailable. Reverting the classifier fails 9 of them.
2. `tests/units/ontology/test_shape_conditions.py`
   New module unit-testing `ShapeConditions` directly: the shared decision-table
   operator vocabulary, `get_conditions` dropping unusable rows, every SQL
   operator including string case folding and quote escaping, LEFT JOIN and
   existence subquery forms, the in-memory matcher (multi-valued properties,
   non-numeric objects under a numeric comparison, and/or logic), and the SPARQL
   target (full IRIs, OPTIONAL under `or`, BIND(EXISTS) under `or`, escaping).
   A cross-check asserts all three back ends can translate every operator.
3. `tests/units/ontology/test_dataquality.py`
   Add `TestConditionPersistence`: a shape is unconditional by default, an
   unknown logic falls back to `and`, and clearing conditions through
   `update_shape` restores the unwrapped query.

