--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Grant the app service principal CAN_MANAGE_RUN on the graph analytics job
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Context:
Running the KG analysis in the deployed app failed with "Databricks job
'ontobricks-07x-graph-analytics' was not found. Deploy the bundle (make deploy)
so the graph analytics job exists." — but the bundle had deployed the job
correctly (`[dev benoit_cayla] ontobricks-07x-graph-analytics`, id
334933950622420).

Root cause: the job ACL only contained the owner (benoit.cayla) and `admins`.
`LakeflowRunner.resolve_job_id()` locates the job with `jobs.list()`, which is
ACL-filtered, so the app's service principal (dde41394-…) saw no job at all and
the lookup reported "not found". `scripts/bootstrap/app-permissions.sh` grants
app-self CAN_MANAGE, cross-app CAN_USE and UC schema ALL_PRIVILEGES, but never
granted anything on the analytics job — so every fresh deploy hit this. The
grant cannot be declared in DAB because the app service principal only exists
after the app is created, hence the post-deploy bootstrap step.

Changes:

1. `scripts/bootstrap/app-permissions.sh`
   New step: resolve the analytics job by name (exact, then the `[dev <user>] `
   prefixed form DAB produces in development mode) and grant CAN_MANAGE_RUN to
   both app service principals. Skips with a clear message when the job does
   not exist yet; prints the manual command on failure, matching the UC step.
2. `src/back/core/graph_analysis/LakeflowRunner.py`
   The NotFoundError blamed the deploy only. It now names both causes (not
   deployed, or missing CAN_MANAGE_RUN) and points at `make bootstrap-perms`.
   Docstring records that `jobs.list()` is ACL-filtered.
3. `tests/units/core/test_lakeflow_runner.py`
   New test asserting the message names the permission cause, alongside the
   existing "make deploy" assertion.

